, 0.03667633607983589, 0.0098430085927248, -0.01276128739118576, -0.013750534504652023, 0.01124650239944458, -0.025374187156558037, 0.049116116017103195, -0.017497306689620018, 0.03487095981836319, 0.009484406560659409, -0.015605372376739979, 0.01774461939930916, 0.004819488152861595, 0.022183865308761597, -0.007240051869302988, 0.009156717918813229, 0.04041074216365814, 0.012532523833215237, -0.032051604241132736, 0.003412902355194092, -0.006204433739185333, -0.009496771730482578, 0.008989783003926277, 0.036874182522296906, 0.009750266559422016, 0.024694079533219337, -0.04211719334125519, -0.013837093487381935, -0.02849031612277031, -0.03571181744337082, 0.00950295478105545, 0.010251072235405445, 0.03301611915230751, 0.003963171038776636, -0.0038549720775336027, 0.0034190851729363203, 0.031186014413833618]], "input": ["QUILOMICRONES:QUILOMICRONES are large lipoprotein particles that originate in the intestine and are primarily composed of triglycerides. They play a crucial role in the transport of dietary lipids, including triglycerides and other fats, from the intestines to various tissues throughout the body. Additionally, quilomicrones can be involved in conditions such as hyperlipidemia, which is characterized by elevated levels of lipids in the blood. Overall, quilomicrones are essential for lipid transport and metabolism within the body.", "APO B-100:Apolipoprotein B-100 (Apo B-100) is a crucial protein involved in the metabolism of low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and is recognized by LDL receptors. It serves as the primary apolipoprotein of LDL, playing a vital role in lipid metabolism and transport.", "APO B-48:Apolipoprotein B-48 is the main apolipoprotein of VLDL, crucial for the metabolism of lipoproteins.", "LIPOPROTEINA LIPASA (LPL):Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is an enzyme that hydrolyzes triglycerides in lipoproteins, facilitating the release of free fatty acids to tissues.", "TEJIDO ADIPOSO:TEJIDO ADIPOSO, or adipose tissue, is a type of connective tissue that serves as a crucial storage site for fat, playing a significant role in energy metabolism. It is involved in the storage and metabolism of energy, with its functions being influenced by the gut microbiota and various signaling molecules. Adipose tissue receives fatty acids released from lipoproteins, further contributing to its role in lipid storage and overall energy balance in the body.", "MUSCULO ESQUELETICO:Skeletal muscle is a site of utilization for fatty acids released from lipoproteins.", "IDLS:Intermediate-density lipoproteins (IDLs) are formed during the metabolism of VLDL and are involved in lipid transport.", "LDLS:Low-density lipoproteins (LDLs) are the final product of the VLDL-IDL-LDL metabolic cascade, primarily transporting cholesterol to cells.", "HDLS:High-density lipoproteins (HDLs) are involved in reverse cholesterol transport and are synthesized from the catabolism of VLDL.", "VLDL:VLDL, or Very Low-Density Lipoprotein, are a type of lipoprotein produced by the liver. They play a crucial role in lipid metabolism by transporting triglycerides and cholesterol from the liver to various tissues in the body. VLDL is particularly associated with conditions such as combined familial hyperlipidemia, which is characterized by elevated levels of lipids in the blood. Overall, VLDL are essential for the distribution of lipids throughout the body, facilitating the delivery of energy and structural components to cells.", "APOLIPOPROTEINAS:Apolipoproteins are proteins that bind lipids to form lipoproteins, playing critical roles in lipid metabolism.", "TRIGLICERIDOS:Trigliceridos, also known as triglycerides, are a type of fat found in the blood that play a crucial role in energy storage and metabolism. They are the main type of fat in the body and serve as a significant energy source, as well as being a component of lipid solutions and lipoproteins. The levels of triglycerides are particularly important to monitor in patients receiving lipid infusions during parenteral nutrition, highlighting their relevance in clinical settings. Overall, triglycerides are essential for various bodily functions, including energy provision and the formation of important biological molecules.", "LIPOPROTEINAS:", "ÁCIDO NICOTÍNICO:Ácido nicotínico, also known as nicotinic acid, is a medication that has been utilized for over 40 years in the treatment of dyslipidemia. It primarily functions by decreasing the production and release of very low-density lipoprotein (VLDL), which is crucial for managing lipid levels in the body. Additionally, nicotinic acid can be used in conjunction with fibrates to effectively lower triglyceride levels, further supporting its role in lipid management.", "MANUAL DE NUTRICIÓN:The Manual de Nutrición is a publication that includes guidelines and information on nutrition and metabolism, published by the Sociedad Española de Endocrinología y Nutrición.", "AACE/ACE:The AACE/ACE refers to the American Association of Clinical Endocrinology and the American College of Endocrinology, organizations that provide guidelines for managing dyslipidemia and cardiovascular disease."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "temperature": 0.0, "frequency_penalty": null, "presence_penalty": null, "top_p": 1.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1}, "history": null}